// A name written on its own reaches what this module holds, so a name that
// reaches nothing here names nothing at all. Naming another module's type
// takes the module: `a.Thing`, or `use a.Thing` and then the word.
//
// Reaching for any binding of that name anywhere in the program made this
// compile. The checker answers such a token with poison and reports nothing,
// because it believes the resolver already did -- so the annotation resolved
// to no type, `peek` lowered to a constant, and the program ran returning 0
// where 7 is the answer. Adding `use a.Thing` is what makes the name reach
// something, and is what this file leaves out.
//
// A pattern's head is the other rule: it cannot be qualified, so there the
// wide search is right. That is a separate case.
import a

fn peek(t: Thing) -> UInt8 {
    t.n
}

fn main() -> UInt8 {
    peek(a.make())
}
